  finalFile: `// Assets/Scripts/Card_data.cs — the whole file, as it stands after this lesson.
// It SHIPS with the project. The one line marked " + " is yours; the rest was
// already here and is not retyped.

using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

[CreateAssetMenu(fileName = "Card_data", menuName = "Cards/Card_data", order = 1)]
public class Card_data : ScriptableObject
{
    public string card_name;
    public string description;
    public int health;
    public int cost;
    public int damage;
    public Sprite sprite;
    public int fontSize = 36;
    public string rarity;                 // + added by you
}

// Assets/Scripts/Card.cs — additions only, and only the parts that changed.
// The other fifty lines of the file are already there. Do not retype them.

    public string rarity;                 // + with the other value copies
    public TextMeshProUGUI rarityText;    // + with the other TMP references

    void Start()
    {
        card_name = data.card_name;
        description = data.description;
        health = data.health;
        cost = data.cost;
        damage = data.damage;
        sprite = data.sprite;
        rarity = data.rarity;             // + beside the other six copies
        nameText.text = card_name;
        nameText.fontSize = data.fontSize;
        descriptionText.text = description;
        healthText.text = health.ToString();
        costText.text = cost.ToString();
        damageText.text = damage.ToString();
        spriteImage.sprite = sprite;
        rarityText.text = rarity;         // + a string, so no .ToString()
    }

// Inspector changes made in this lesson:
//   Assets/Scripts/Card_data.cs : + public string rarity;
//   Assets/Scripts/Card.cs      : + rarity, + rarityText, + two lines in Start()
//   Blank_Card.prefab           : + one duplicated text object (Ctrl+D on Damage,
//                                   renamed Rarity, moved into place)
//                               : + Card component ▸ Rarity Text wired to it
//   Assets/Cards/Card_Data/     : + your card assets (five today, 15-20 tonight)
//   GameManager (the Dealer)    : + player_deck filled with those assets`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare against your own two files and your prefab. The three things to check hardest: that `rarity` exists in BOTH files, that the copy line is `rarity = data.rarity;` and not `rarity = rarity;`, and that the Rarity Text slot on the Card component is not empty.',
};

const body = `<h1>10.2b — ScriptableObjects: Card Data as Assets</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Yesterday your deck became a <code>List&lt;Card_data&gt;</code>. Today you find out what a
    <code>Card_data</code> actually is: not a class you create in code, but a FILE — an asset
    you create in the Project window, fill in with the mouse, and drag into that list. That is
    the difference between adding a card by editing a script and adding a card by clicking.
    Your project has two of these assets already, and by the end of today it will have fifteen.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I make game data into something I can drag?', 1)}

${renderDecompose('10.2b', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The ScriptableObject, defined.</strong> It is a data
    class that becomes a FILE in your project (an asset, like a
    sprite). The template already ships one — open
    <code>Assets/Scripts/Card_data.cs</code> and read it:</p>
    <pre><code>[CreateAssetMenu(fileName = "Card_data", menuName = "Cards/Card_data", order = 1)]
public class Card_data : ScriptableObject
{
    public string card_name;
    public string description;
    public int health;
    public int cost;
    public int damage;
    public Sprite sprite;
    public int fontSize = 36;
}</code></pre>
    <p>Each field is one row of a card, and each ASSET you create from
    this class is one card, editable in the Inspector. Right-click in
    Assets &rarr; Create &rarr; Cards &rarr; Card_data — and an .asset file
    appears. Make FIVE cards. Open them in the Inspector: fields, not
    code. Designers edit these; programmers only build the shape once.
    Build It With Me below takes every line of that class apart, so read
    the file now and know where each piece is.</p>

    <p><strong>Your project already has two cards, and together they
    show you both ends of the pipeline.</strong> Open
    <code>Assets/Cards/Card_Data/</code> and compare them side by side.
    <code>Orc_Data.asset</code> is filled in: a name, a description,
    health, cost, damage and a sprite. <code>Unicorn_data.asset</code>
    is empty in every single field — a card that was created and never
    written. Same class, same Inspector, two very different amounts of
    work.</p>

    <p>Now find the last thing in <code>Assets/Scripts/Card.cs</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>if (card_name == "Orc")
{
    background.color = Color.chocolate;
}</code></pre>
    <p>That is where an asset meets the code. The card's name is not a
    string somebody typed into a script — it is <code>data.card_name</code>,
    copied onto the card by <code>Start()</code>, and "Orc" is exactly what
    the Orc asset says. Rename that asset in the Inspector and this branch
    stops running. Data you can edit with the mouse just decided what the
    code does.</p>

    <p><strong>Compare your three data homes so far:</strong></p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Data lives in…</th><th>Example you built</th><th>Edited by</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Serialized fields on a component</td><td>the <code>playerTransform</code> reference wired in the Inspector (<a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>)</td><td>Inspector, per scene object</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scene/prefab YAML</td><td>everything (<a href="9.4.html">9.4</a>)</td><td>Unity (and you, by hand, once)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ScriptableObject assets</td><td>your cards</td><td>Inspector, in the PROJECT, reusable everywhere</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The two deck Lists hold <code>Card_data</code> assets; the
            four hand and discard Lists hold <code>Card</code> objects.
            When you drag the SAME card asset into three of those
            Lists, how many copies of the card exist in memory — and
            why is "reference, not copy" exactly what
            <a href="9.4.html">9.4</a> guids do for sprites?
            (One card asset, many references. The exam's word is
            abstraction — again.)
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the four lines you type.</strong> Build It With
    Me above takes <code>Assets/Scripts/Card_data.cs</code> apart line by
    line, adds your own field to it, then mirrors that field into
    <code>Assets/Scripts/Card.cs</code> and gives it a text object to
    appear in. Both files already exist in this project and both already
    work — you are extending them, never re-creating them. If you have not
    done that block yet, do it now: the rest of this lab assumes a card
    can show a field you invented.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — author five cards.</strong> In the Project window,
    right-click &rarr; Create &rarr; Cards &rarr; Card_data. The new file
    arrives called <code>Card_data</code>, so rename it to the card before
    you do anything else or your project fills up with
    <code>Card_data 1</code>, <code>Card_data 2</code> and no way to tell
    them apart in a deck list. Fill each one in the Inspector, keep them
    together in <code>Assets/Cards/Card_Data/</code> beside the two that
    ship, and give each card a number that makes it different from the
    others — a card that costs the same and hits the same as every other
    card is not a card, it is the same card wearing a different name.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — put them in the deck.</strong> No new field, and no
    code. <code>GameManager.cs</code> already declares the list the
    Inspector edits:</p>
    <pre><code>public List&lt;Card_data&gt; player_deck = new List&lt;Card_data&gt;();</code></pre>
    <p>Select the object carrying GameManager in the card scene and
    <code>player_deck</code> is waiting: an element count, then one slot per
    card. Drag your assets in. Duplicate entries are legal — three copies of
    your best card is a design decision that costs three slots and one asset
    file. Read the slots that are already there before you fill them: a slot
    showing <code>None</code> is a null reference sitting in the list, and
    <code>Deal()</code> reads <code>player_deck[0]</code> and hands whatever
    it finds straight to <code>Card.Start()</code>, which reads
    <code>data.card_name</code> on the first frame. An empty slot is not a
    harmless one.</p>

    <p>Then notice how little <code>Deal()</code> does with all that work:
    it instantiates one card per side and reads index <code>[0]</code>. A
    deck of twenty and a deck of one look identical on screen today — you
    are stocking a list that the shuffle in <a href="10.2c.html">10.2c</a>
    is going to need before it can mean anything.</p>

    <p><strong>And read one more line while you are in there.</strong> The
    last line of <code>Deal()</code> turns <code>DraggableUI</code> off on
    the player's card and leaves it on for the AI's. The drag behaviour
    ships with the project, and the scene ships the Canvas and EventSystem
    that UI dragging needs. Which card gets to be dragged, though, is a
    decision somebody made — read the line and decide whether it is a bug or
    a rule, and say which one you would have written.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — prove the pipeline.</strong> Add a card WITHOUT
    touching code: create, fill, drag into the deck, play. If that took
    under two minutes, your content pipeline works and the rest of the jam
    is content rather than plumbing. The proof is not that a new card
    exists — it is that you never opened a script to make it.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Data design traps</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Nothing in the project READS <code>health</code>,
                <code>cost</code> or <code>damage</code> yet — the
                numbers go straight from the asset into a text
                label. Which of the three will your game's rules
                read first, and what does the exam call the
                discipline of matching the data you keep to the job
                it does (<a href="5.1.html">5.1</a>: right-size the
                data)?</li>
            <li>You EDIT a card asset during Play mode. Press stop.
                What survived — and why is asset data MORE
                dangerous to edit than scene data at runtime?</li>
            <li>Two cards share one art sprite. You delete the
                sprite file. What did <a href="9.4.html">9.4</a>
                teach you happens — and how many cards broke?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

